e case of gender pronoun resolution, this is achieved by removing the path between gender and context. Further, because W is also gender-neutral, once we have removed any gender-identifying features from context, we should additionally remove the path between context and gender-pronouns, as there is no longer any feature of X causing Y.\nWith the causal path from X \u2192 Y removed, had there been no backdoor path from X to Y, then we would then expect X and Y to be unconditionally independent in the real world (RW ). However, in the scenario depicted in Figure 1(b), selection collider bias has opened the backdoor path, X \u2190W \u2192 S \u2190 G\u2192 Y , Thus, the learned model (LM ) is encouraged to learn any spurious association along this path that reduces predictive risk, resulting in X and Y becoming unconditionally dependent. Formally, selection collider bias causes the transformation: (Y \u22a5\u22a5X)RW\ns\u21d2 (Y \u22a5\u0338\u22a5X)LM, due to even very weakly interacting spurious associations, such as those between gender and time. Whereas spurious associations learned in the well-specified model would be likely due to signals that are stronger than those along the causal path X \u2192 Y 1."
